-- title:
--   Lemma 3.5 — stochastic maximum principle for the mean field equilibrium, both directions
-- statement:
--   Suppose that Assumption 3.1 holds.
--
--   1. If $(m,\alpha)$ is a MFE with state process $X$, then
--   $$\alpha_t=\hat\alpha(X_t,Y_t,Z_t,Z^0_t,m_t)\qquad dt\otimes d\mathbb P\text{-a.e.}\quad(3.8)$$
--   for some solution $(X,Y,Z,Z^0)$ of the conditional McKean–Vlasov FBSDE (3.6).
--   2. Suppose in addition that $x\mapsto G(x,m)$ is convex for each $m$, that $(x,a)\mapsto b(x,a,m)\cdot y+\sigma(x,a,m)\cdot z+\sigma^0(x,a,m)\cdot z^0+L(x,a,m)$ is convex for each fixed $(y,z,z^0,m)$, and that $\hat\alpha$ has linear growth in $(x,y,z,z^0,m)$. If $(X,Y,Z,Z^0)$ is any solution of (3.6), then $(m,\alpha)$ with $m_t=\mathcal L(X_t\mid\mathcal F^0_t)$ and $\alpha_t=\hat\alpha(X_t,Y_t,Z_t,Z^0_t,m_t)$ is a MFE: some version $m'$ of the conditional law is $\mathbb F^0$-adapted with continuous paths, and $(m',\hat\alpha(X,Y,Z,Z^0,m'))$ is a MFE with state process $X$.
--
--   The lemma reduces the mean field equilibrium to the McKean–Vlasov FBSDE (3.6), on which the convergence argument works.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: the canonical spaces $\Omega^N=(\mathbb R^n)^N\times\mathcal C^{N+1}$ with product measure, embedded in one another (pp.9–10) → one probability space carrying $\xi^1,\xi^2,\dots$ (law $m_0$), $W^0$ and $W^1,W^2,\dots$, mutually independent, Brownian paths continuous (`Setup`); reason: the canonical product space is an instance, so the statement specializes to the paper's
--   - generalization: $d,k,n\in\mathbb N=\{1,2,\dots\}$ → any $n,k,d\ge 0$; reason: the source's dimensions are instances
--   - encoding: augmented filtrations $\mathbb F^N$, $\mathbb F^0$ → the raw filtrations generated by $\xi^1,\dots,\xi^N,W^0,\dots,W^N$ and by $W^0$; reason: every process progressive for the augmented filtration has a raw-progressive version ([41, Prop. 1.2.1], cited p.10); controls and states enter only up to versions
--   - encoding: continuous processes → processes with almost surely continuous paths on $[0,T]$; reason: on a general probability space continuity holds outside a null set; suprema and integrals do not see null sets
--   - encoding: $\mathbb R^{n\times d}$ with the Frobenius norm and $A\cdot B=\mathrm{tr}(A^\top B)$ → `EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n × Fin d)`; reason: same norm and inner product
--   - encoding: $J^{N,i}$, $J_m$ real valued (footnote 1, p.12) → the costs of the equilibrium are finite, and only deviations with finite (integrable) cost are compared; reason: under the paper's assumptions every cost is finite, so the compared sets coincide
--   - encoding: $m_t=\mathcal L(X_t\mid\mathcal F^0_t)$ → $\omega\mapsto m_t(\omega)(A)$ is a.s. equal to $\mathbb P[X_t\in A\mid\mathcal F^0_t]$ for every Borel $A$ (`IsCondLaw`); $\mathbb F^0$-adaptedness of $m$ through the evaluation maps; reason: this is the definition of a version of the conditional law
--   - encoding: §2.3: Fréchet differentiability of the lift $\xi\mapsto U(x,\mathcal L(\xi))$ on $\mathbb L^2(\Omega)$, with continuous $D_mU$ → the published `DisplMonoMFG.WellPosed.HasLDeriv` (first-order expansion uniform over the joint laws of $(\xi,\eta)$ with $\mathcal L(\xi)=m$), plus joint continuity; versions of $D_m$ as data (`Derivs`); reason: on an atomless space the two notions coincide; a Lions derivative is determined only $m$-a.e., and every statement evaluates it on the support
--   - encoding: "Lipschitz in all arguments", "bounded", "$C^1$" for maps on $\mathbb R^p\times\mathcal P_2(\mathbb R^n)$ → `LipXM`, `BddXM`, `IsC1` with $\mathcal W_2$ in the measure and the max-norm on the product of the other arguments; reason: equivalent norms give the same notions up to the (existential) constants
--   - encoding: "for any square-integrable random variables" in (1.6), (2.16), (2.19) → for every coupling $\pi$ with finite second moments, the laws being the marginals of $\pi$, whenever the integrand is integrable; reason: random variables enter only through their joint law, and every coupling is a joint law on an atomless space
--   - encoding: $\hat\alpha$, the unique minimizer of (3.1) → `alphaHat`: a minimizer chosen by `Classical.choose`, `0` if none exists; reason: the minimizer exists and is unique under Assumptions 2.5, 2.12 and 2.19 (p.19)
--   - encoding: $D_yH$, $D_zH$, $D_{z^0}H$, $D_xH$ in the FBSDEs (3.2), (3.6) → their minimizer form $b(x,\hat\alpha,m)$, $\sigma(x,\hat\alpha,m)$, $\sigma^0(x,\hat\alpha,m)$, $D_x[b\cdot y+\sigma\cdot z+\sigma^0\cdot z^0](x,\hat\alpha,m)+D_xL(x,\hat\alpha,m)$; reason: equal wherever $H$ is differentiable ($H\le$ the minimized function, with equality at $\hat\alpha$), in particular under Assumption 2.17; it is the form of (3.4), (3.5), (3.9), (3.10) in the other two cases
--   - encoding: backward equations with terminal condition → forward Itô form from the random initial value together with the terminal condition (`IsItoProc` + a.s. terminal value); reason: the integral form of a BSDE
--   - reading: "$\hat\alpha$ has linear growth in $(x,y,z,z^0,m)$" → $|\hat\alpha(x,y,z,z^0,m)|\le C(1+|x|+|y|+|z|+|z^0|+M_2(m)^{1/2})$; reason: the size of $m\in\mathcal P_2$ is $M_2(m)^{1/2}$
--   - encoding: the conditional law $\mathcal L(X_t\mid\mathcal F^0_t)$ inside (3.6) → a version $m$ that is $\mathbb F^0$-progressive (evaluations $\omega\mapsto m_t(\omega)(A)$ progressive); the conclusion produces an $\mathbb F^0$-adapted continuous version $m'$ ("$m$ is a continuous version", proof, p.21) and the MFE is $(m',\hat\alpha(X,Y,Z,Z^0,m'))$; reason: every measure flow of §2.4.2 (p.12) is $\mathbb F^0$-progressive, and progressive versions differ only on $dt\otimes d\mathbb P$-null sets, so the coefficients of (3.6) do not depend on the version
--   - encoding: "for some solution $(X,Y,Z,Z^0)$ of (3.6)" → a solution whose forward component is the MFE state process $X$; reason: the forward component solves the state equation of $\alpha$, whose solution is unique
-- source:
--   Jackson & Tangpi, Quantitative Convergence for Displacement Monotone Mean Field Games with Controlled Volatility, arXiv:2304.04543v2, Lemma 3.5 and its proof, p.21; (3.6), (3.7) p.20

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_CtrlVolMFG_IsSol_3_6

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped ENNReal NNReal

namespace CtrlVolMFG

/-- Lemma 3.5 (Jackson & Tangpi, arXiv:2304.04543v2, p.21). Suppose that Assumption 3.1 holds.
1. If `(m, α)` is a MFE with state process `X`, then `α_t = α̂(X_t, Y_t, Z_t, Z⁰_t, m_t)`
   `dt × dℙ`-a.e. (3.8) for some solution `(X, Y, Z, Z⁰)` of (3.6).
2. Suppose in addition that `x ↦ G(x, m)` is convex for each `m`, that
   `(x, a) ↦ b(x, a, m)·y + σ(x, a, m)·z + σ⁰(x, a, m)·z⁰ + L(x, a, m)` is convex for each fixed
   `(y, z, z⁰, m)`, and that `α̂` has linear growth in `(x, y, z, z⁰, m)`. If `(X, Y, Z, Z⁰)` is any
   solution of (3.6), then `(m, α)` with `m_t = ℒ(X_t | ℱ⁰_t)` and
   `α_t = α̂(X_t, Y_t, Z_t, Z⁰_t, m_t)` is a MFE (with state process `X`): some version `m'` of the
   conditional law is `𝔽⁰`-adapted with continuous paths (the proof: "`m` is a continuous version")
   and `(m', α̂(X, Y, Z, Z⁰, m'))` is a MFE.

**Formalization Note** Linear growth in `m` is growth in `M₂(m)^{1/2}`. The version `m` of
`ℒ(X_t | ℱ⁰_t)` with which the solution of (3.6) is written is taken `𝔽⁰`-progressive (as every
measure flow of §2.4.2, p.12), so that versions differ only on `dt × dℙ`-null sets. -/
theorem lemma_3_5 {n k d : ℕ} {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (S : Setup n d Ω)
    (D : Data n k d) (V : Derivs n k d) (T : ℝ≥0) (hT : 0 < T) (h31 : Assumption_3_1 D V T) :
    (∀ (m : ℝ≥0 → Ω → P2 n) (α : ℝ≥0 → Ω → Rn k) (X : ℝ≥0 → Ω → Rn n), IsMFE S D T m α X →
      ∃ (Y : ℝ≥0 → Ω → Rn n) (Z Z0 : ℝ≥0 → Ω → Mat n d), IsSol_3_6 S D T m X Y Z Z0 ∧
        EqDtDP S.P T α (fun t ω => D.alphaHat (X t ω) (Y t ω) (Z t ω) (Z0 t ω) (m t ω))) ∧
    ((∀ m, ConvexOn ℝ Set.univ (fun x => D.G x m)) →
      (∀ y z z0 m, ConvexOn ℝ Set.univ (fun q : Rn n × Rn k => D.ham q.1 q.2 y z z0 m)) →
      (∃ C : ℝ, ∀ x y z z0 m, ‖D.alphaHat x y z z0 m‖ ≤
        C * (1 + ‖x‖ + ‖y‖ + ‖z‖ + ‖z0‖ + Real.sqrt (M2 m))) →
      ∀ (m : ℝ≥0 → Ω → P2 n) (X Y : ℝ≥0 → Ω → Rn n) (Z Z0 : ℝ≥0 → Ω → Mat n d),
        IsSol_3_6 S D T m X Y Z Z0 →
        (∀ A : Set (Rn n), MeasurableSet A →
          IsStronglyProgressive S.F0 (fun t ω => ((m t ω).1 A).toReal)) →
        ∃ m' : ℝ≥0 → Ω → P2 n,
          IsMFE S D T m' (fun t ω => D.alphaHat (X t ω) (Y t ω) (Z t ω) (Z0 t ω) (m' t ω)) X) := by sorry

end CtrlVolMFG
